# shir_nodes/str_splice.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# StrSplice(base, start, end, repl): the value of a
# `${b:0:$start}${repl}${b:$end}` interpolation — b's prefix [0,start),
# then repl, then b's suffix from `end` — with BOTH slice offsets clamped
# exactly as bash clamps `${x:off:len}` (negative `end` counts back from
# the end; a negative `start` drops that many trailing bytes). Folded from
# that Interpolate shape by `str-splice-fold`
# (transforms/str_splice_fold.rs), which only fires when both slices read
# the SAME base and the suffix runs to the end of the string.
#
# Why a node instead of the concat it replaced: the concat form forces
# every backend to build TWO substring temps plus a join temp, which the
# store then copies a fourth time — ~4 passes over the whole string per
# store (the per-pixel row rebuild in MIMEcroft's rasterizer: 2 substr +
# snprintf + array copy over a 2560-byte row for EVERY pixel). A node
# lets the destination's store write the result STRAIGHT INTO its own
# buffer when it fits (capacity-guarded, one pass) while remaining
# exactly the concat it replaced for every other consumer.
#
# Backends: C (capacity-guarded fused store — target-gated offer, PLAN
# §11 marketplace: a backend accepts by adding the value/store arms and
# its target in transforms::transform_applies). Targets that do not run
# the fold never see the node: they keep the original Interpolate.
# Fallback expansion: the exact Interpolate this was folded from
# (renderers may always rebuild it — the C value-position arm does).
#
# `base` is an EXPR field holding the plain `Var` read of the base
# string (not a bare name string): the analysis walkers recurse
# `ExtExpr::children`, so the base read counts as an ordinary read
# (dead_store_elim's census — a `string` field would have hidden it and
# let DSE drop the base's only store).
#
node StrSplice
tag "StrSplice"
kind expr
field base: expr
field start: expr
field end: expr
field repl: expr
spec: none (unstated)
